{
  "$comment": [
    "Shared citation-key contract between lit-downloader and word-bibtex-citation-addin (BibCite).",
    "Canonical in reference-manager's bib-core/, vendored byte-identical at tests/citekey-conformance.json",
    "in lit-downloader and BibCite alike, each re-synced by its own npm run sync:bib-core. Keep all three",
    "in lockstep, never one alone, and run both suites before landing: BibCite refuses a whole",
    "library over one bad key, and lit-downloader's parser runs on every save, so any",
    "disagreement means one tool silently stops working on files the other accepts. Copy this",
    "file, not the regex: two hand-maintained copies of a literal drift, and one already did.",
    "accept: the key is valid under its exact text, parsed and indexed verbatim by",
    "lit-downloader, accepted into a citation tag by BibCite.",
    "reject: the key is never valid under this exact text. BibCite rejects every such key",
    "outright. For lit-downloader's file parser, `mode` says which behaviour BibTeX's own",
    "grammar requires: 'refuse' means the character cannot be read back at all, so the whole",
    "file is refused; 'terminate' means the character ENDS the key, so the shorter key in",
    "`reads` is parsed instead (BibTeX reads the same shorter key; refusing would reject",
    "valid files).",
    "Invisible rejected characters appear as \\u escapes, by design.",
    "Two kinds of character are barred beyond the structural ones. INVISIBLE characters take no",
    "width, so they hide or reorder what surrounds them. BLANK GLYPHS take width and draw",
    "nothing, so a key can pad itself into the appearance of another. Whitespace is the familiar",
    "blank glyph and BibTeX ends a key at it; U+2800 BRAILLE PATTERN BLANK is the one that is",
    "neither whitespace nor default-ignorable, and closing that gap is why this file grew its",
    "braille cases.",
    "Keys must also already be NFC; cafe plus a combining acute is rejected.",
    "RESERVED NAMES: a key that names a member of JavaScript's Object.prototype (or",
    "'prototype') cannot be held as data by a record keyed on it, so BibCite refuses the",
    "whole library. Both tools now refuse such a key on read, and the writer never emits",
    "one; the cases below are the runtime's own list, spelled out so a host with a",
    "different Object.prototype still agrees with the file."
  ],
